+# Feature Specification: JSON Pointer (RFC 6901)
+
+**Status**: Implemented
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing
+
+### User Story 1 - Resolve a pointer against a document (P1)
+A service developer passes a JSON document and a JSON Pointer string and gets back the referenced value.
+
+**Acceptance scenarios**:
+1. Given any document, when the pointer is `""`, then the whole document is returned.
+2. Given `{"foo":["bar","baz"]}`, `/foo/0` returns `"bar"`.
+3. Given the RFC 6901 §5 example document, every example pointer yields the value listed in the RFC (including `/a~1b`, `/m~0n`, `/ `, `/k"l`, `/c%d`, `/` → key `""`).
+
+### User Story 2 - Failures are reported as errors (P1)
+**Acceptance scenarios**:
+1. A pointer not starting with `/` (other than `""`), or containing `~` not followed by `0` or `1`, throws.
+2. A pointer naming a missing member, an out-of-range index, or traversing into a scalar/null throws.
+3. A non-string pointer throws.
+
+## Requirements
+- **FR-001**: `resolvePointer(document, pointer)` is exported from `src/index.ts`.
+- **FR-002**: Pointer syntax follows RFC 6901 §3: empty string or sequence of `/`-prefixed reference tokens.
+- **FR-003**: Tokens are unescaped per §4: `~1` → `/` first, then `~0` → `~`.
+- **FR-004**: On objects, a token selects an own member by exact name (inherited properties such as `__proto__`, `constructor` never resolve).
+- **FR-005**: On arrays, a token must be `0` or digits with no leading zeros and no sign; it must be less than the array length. `-` (past-the-end) never resolves to a value and throws.
+- **FR-006**: A resolved value of `null` is a valid result and is returned, not treated as missing.
+- **FR-007**: Invalid or unresolvable pointers throw an `Error` with a descriptive message.
+- **FR-008**: The document is not modified.
+
+## Assumptions
+- Only the JSON string form of pointers is supported (not the URI fragment form, §6).
+- Later features (mutation, patch) will reuse the parsing logic, so parsing is exposed as an internal module.
+
+## Success Criteria
+- All RFC 6901 §5 examples resolve correctly; all invalid cases above throw.
